  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \nWe develop a method combining coordinate descent and gradient projection on the simplex. The core idea is to represent the sequence in a band-limited Fourier basis to reduce dimensionality while maintaining the ability to compute gradients. This allows us to handle the non-negativity constraints more effectively and escape local minima through eigenvector-like updates.  \n\nThe mathematical structure of the problem suggests that sequences with concentrated energy might have undesirable autocorrelations, so we distribute energy as much as possible. By iteratively finding sequences with improved smoothness and reducing the Lipschitz constant of the convolution operation, we achieve a lower bound than the parent construction.  \n\nOur algorithm distinguishes between points where the uniform distribution (sum=1) is binding and where it isn't. In the first case, we enforce Lipschitz continuity at extrema, in the second, we apply scaled gradient projections.  \n\n2. Mathematical specification  \nLet a be a sequence of length n. Its Fourier transform representation is:\n\n   a(t) = \u03a3_{k=0}^{K_max} A_k * cos(\u03c9_k * t + \u03c6_k)\n\nwhere \u03c9_k are frequencies derived from simplex projection rules. Each term A_k must satisfy A_k \u2264 2\u221a(small value) for the Lipschitz condition.\n\nThe objective function to minimize is:\n\n   Score = (2 * n * max(convolve(a, a))) / (sum(a))^2\n\nUsing a Fourier basis with up to 5 harmonics ensures both optimality and swift computation. The smoothness of the solution translates to stable, low-score sequences with minimal computational overhead.  \n\n3. Implementation procedure  \nInitialize with the parent sequence (if available) or with a uniform sequence. Then, perform:  \n\n- Step 1: Fourier projection of the sequence onto the simplex.  \n- Step 2: Gradient calculation for the max value in convolution using position-specific analytic approximations.  \n- Step 3: Update individual coordinates with eigenvector-based perturbations.  \n- Step 4: Repeat until a stable sequence is found with score approaching 1.5027.  \n\nInclude self-healing mechanisms by checking periodically against the original objective function and integrating simplex-trimmed updates. Ensure all sequences remain nonnegative and within the evaluated bounds.  \n\n4. Search choices and useful variations  \nWe leverage multiple simplex projections with separate update rules per coordinate type. When processing the sequence, we consider both large variations in coordinates and eigenvector-like updates.  \n\nThe update parameters (\u0394, scaling factors) are adaptive, normally ranging between 0.001 and 0.3 per iteration. This covers:\n\n- Minor tunings for fine improvements  \n- Major adjustments to escape local minima  \n\n5. Budget and robustness  \nWith 1800 seconds allocated, ensure strict enforcement of time:  \n   - Step Fourier projection: 100 seconds  \n   - Step gradient calculation: 50 seconds  \n   - Step update loop (maximum 500 iterations divided per time blocks)  \n\nTotal workers: 3 (projections)+5 (updates)=8 feasible time blocks yielding at worst 8\u00d7500=4000 updates within time bounds.  \n\n6. Validation and return contract  \n- All sequences must be nonnegative and have sum at least 0.01  \n- Target: score \u2264 1.5027  \n- Implemented with a fallback evaluation using np.inf if invalid  \n\nReturn the sequence in a list format. Validate before submission.",
